🤖 Agent: That’s a meaningful research project — and there are several concrete sources and steps you can take to learn more about your great‑aunt’s refugee experience and the post‑WWII refugee/resettlement process. Below is a practical plan, key resources, and what to gather to make requests more effective.

1) Gather everything you already know (this will speed searches)
- Full name (including any maiden or alternate spellings), date/place of birth, parents’ names if known
- Approximate years or date of emigration to the U.S., port of arrival, ship name (if known)
- Any U.S. documents you or family have: naturalization certificate, passport application, Social Security application (SS‑5), death certificate, marriage certificate, immigration papers, old letters, photos with annotations
- Names of relatives who sponsored or met her in the U.S., and any towns/cities she lived in

2) Start with U.S. immigration / naturalization records
- USCIS Genealogy Program — request naturalization and immigration files (N‑400, N‑600, A‑file). USCIS has a “genealogy” request process for historical records.
- National Archives (NARA) — passenger arrival lists (ship manifests) for ports like New York (Ellis Island) or other ports; NARA also holds records for displaced persons admitted under U.S. programs.
- Social Security Administration (SS‑5) — the SS‑5 application often lists birth, parents’ names, and place of birth. You can request a copy.

3) Displaced persons / refugee–specific resources
- Arolsen Archives (International Tracing Service) — huge collection on displaced persons, DP camps, refugees after WWII. You can submit an inquiry for individual records; they sometimes take time but are very valuable.
- U.S. Department of State / ORR history — background on refugee admission programs (for context on how/when refugees were processed).
- Records of resettlement agencies: HIAS, International Rescue Committee, Church World Service — these organizations handled many refugee arrivals; some have archival records or can help with family tracing.

4) Holocaust / wartime archives (if relevant)
- U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum (USHMM) — searchable collections, testimonies, DP camp and transport records; staff can help with research queries.
- Yad Vashem and JewishGen (if there is a Jewish family connection) — passenger lists, testimonies, and community records.
- Red Cross tracing (International Committee of the Red Cross) — tracing service for wartime separations; they have archives and tracing help.

5) German or local civil records
- If you know the town of birth, contact the local Standesamt (civil registry) or parish (church) records for birth/baptism records. Many German towns’ archives can respond to queries (often in German).
- Bundesarchiv (German Federal Archives) for some wartime/DP records and military or civil records.

6) Newspapers, local histories, and oral sources
- Search local newspapers in the U.S. (where she settled) for arrival notices, naturalization announcements, obituaries — these often give valuable biographical details.
- Ask family members for oral histories, letters, photos, and the names of sponsors or friends — these can point to archives or passenger manifests.
- Local historical societies or ethnic community centers (German clubs, churches) may have files or leads.

7) DNA and family‑history tools
- If you’ve done (or are willing to do) autosomal DNA testing (AncestryDNA, 23andMe, FamilyTreeDNA), results can connect you to relatives and living descendants who might have more documentation or memories.
- Combine DNA matches with genealogical records (Ancestry, FamilySearch) to build branches and trace migration paths.

8) Practical research tips & caveats
- Spelling variants and name changes are common — search alternate spellings, anglicized names, and maiden names.
- Privacy rules: some recent records have access restrictions; older records are more available.
- Use exact date ranges to narrow searches, and keep careful notes of searches and request numbers.
- Many archives have online guides — use them before emailing or submitting requests.

9) If you’d like, I can help you next with:
- Drafting a concise query letter/form for USCIS, Arolsen, or NARA
- A checklist of documents and search terms to use on Ancestry/FamilySearch
- A template email to send to local German civil registries (in English — I can also suggest German phrasing)

